For Experiment 10: Trigger a Jenkins Build Using a GitHub Webhook, we'll connect your GitHub repository `gitexpp3` to Jenkins so that every push to GitHub can trigger a build automatically.

You need Jenkins installed and running on your laptop. If you're using Windows, Jenkins can run locally, but GitHub cannot directly reach `localhost`; we'll address that when configuring the webhook.

### Step 1: Start Jenkins

* Open your browser and visit `http://localhost:8080`.

* Sign in to Jenkins.

* If Jenkins isn't installed, download it from [the official Jenkins website](https://www.jenkins.io/download/).

### Step 2: Create a new Jenkins job

![Jenkins Build Jobs - How to create and trigger build jobs in Jenkins?](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/nVeJNk6dHbW7Hab0GMy2EV9XPqri24bApvjF1Wrm3WSyZJy6d01o6l2cinFy07mNBHGUVrTaUUmOs-50fZoysruCsouy32_9wF8VP5b7ZTxi3EhVqJUaYOFHDolVa7g9FopBb_sUlCMtn2FRp3WBCEAAf8WE5pJRNursqPC32IDsgz3QlZY-CoUAQoQOZKIB?purpose=fullsize)

* Click New Item.

* Enter the job name `GitHub-Auto-Build`.

* Select Freestyle project.

* Click OK.

### Step 3: Connect your GitHub repository

Under Source Code Management:

* Select Git.

* Enter your repository URL:

```text
https://github.com/djaravindsk77-create/gitexpp3.git
```

* For a public repository, you can usually leave Credentials as `- none -`.

* Set the branch to:

```text
*/main
```

### Step 4: Enable the webhook trigger

Under Build Triggers, check:

`GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling`

### Step 5: Add a build step

Under Build Steps, click Add build step.

On Windows, select Execute Windows batch command and enter:

```bat
echo GitHub webhook build started
echo Build successful
```

Click Save.

### Step 6: Configure the GitHub webhook

![How to Integrate Jenkins with GitHub - DevOpslover](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/m5mr8FAqwoTSyxR25fvFmzK1jCqK8K2NBVhTis5oT7MQez0s2vPtjzexph6SwRCqnMvw1N7qF_J2G5qMbBS7iHTt_0KI_ofl-IJrdTpMdWp1IS0X_F_eETGpFIM2KQsw_iNuXk9HvDK0FPeKsmUu_vIJGfZ8c7qlA-ZH5nIZZuVNKfXlAd5AH1F93msasap1?purpose=fullsize)

Open [your GitHub repository](https://github.com/djaravindsk77-create/gitexpp3).

* Go to Settings → Webhooks.

* Click Add webhook.

* Enter the following:

| Field        | Value                                          |
| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Payload URL  | http://YOUR-PUBLIC-JENKINS-URL/github-webhook/ |
| Content type | application/json                               |
| Events       | Just the push event                            |
| Active       | Checked                                        |

4\. Click Add webhook.

Important: Replace `YOUR-PUBLIC-JENKINS-URL` with the publicly reachable address of your Jenkins server. `http://localhost:8080/github-webhook/` will not work for GitHub webhooks because GitHub cannot access your laptop's localhost. For a local lab, you need a properly secured tunnel or public server. Don't expose Jenkins directly to the internet without appropriate security.

### Step 7: Test the integration

Once the webhook can reach Jenkins, make a change in your local repository and push it:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Test Jenkins webhook"
git push origin main
```

### Step 8: Verify the build

* Open Jenkins.

* Select `GitHub-Auto-Build`.

* Check Build History.

* Click the latest build and open Console Output.

Expected output:

```text
GitHub webhook build started
Build successful
Finished: SUCCESS
```

If the build runs automatically after the push, your webhook integration is working.

### All important steps in short

* Start Jenkins.

* Create a Freestyle project.

* Connect the GitHub repository.

* Enable GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling.

* Add a Windows batch build step.

* Configure the GitHub webhook.

* Push a change to `main`.

* Verify the build in Jenkins.

Result: A Jenkins job was configured to build automatically when changes were pushed to the GitHub repository through a webhook, demonstrating continuous integration.
